a tactical operator (phonetically }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid556457\charrsid556457 "a SEAL"}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid556457\charrsid556457 
) would first have to rectify the "loose string" boundary condition to ensure any energy transfer occurs at all.
\par In British English, at an Applied Science (HBO+) level, the analysis of how an operator would maximise the performance of this toy system follows below.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid556457\charrsid556457 1. Rectifying the Boundary Conditions (The String)
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid556457\charrsid556457 The "loose string" described in your previous query presents a catastrophic failure in }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid556457\charrsid556457 energy storage}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid556457\charrsid556457 . A bow functions as a spring system storing elastic potential energy (\\(E_\{p\}\\)).
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \f3\fs20\kerning2\insrsid556457\charrsid556457 \loch\af3\dbch\af31506\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi-360\li720\ri0\sa160\sl278\slmult1\widctlpar
