\insrsid556457\charrsid556457  The suction cup is a flat-faced cylinder, acting as a }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid556457\charrsid556457 blunt body}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid556457\charrsid556457 
 in the airflow. This creates a massive turbulent wake and high pressure drag.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \f3\fs20\kerning2\insrsid556457\charrsid556457 \loch\af3\dbch\af31506\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid556457\charrsid556457 Centre of Pressure (CoP):}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid556457\charrsid556457  
The wide frontal area shifts the Centre of Pressure forward. For stable flight, the Centre of Mass (CoM) must remain ahead of the CoP. Fortunately, the heavy rubber cup keeps the CoM forward, providing static stability, but the high drag causes velocity (
v) to decay exponentially. [}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid556457\charrsid556457 HYPERLINK "https://www.bow-international.com/features/arrows-how-to-minimise-drag-and-maximise-lift/"}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 
